<p>&#8220;But is this actually philosophy?&#8221;<span id="more-58296"></span></p>
<p><a href="https://ethanlandes.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ethan Landes</a> (Eindhoven University of Technology) is tired of experimental philosophers being asked <em>The Question</em>.</p>
<p>And he&#8217;s tired of experimental philosophers &#8220;appeasing&#8221; those who ask it by &#8220;dressing up experimental scholarship up as relevant to armchair analytic philosophy.&#8221;</p>
<div id="attachment_58301" style="width: 769px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-58301" class=" wp-image-58301" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/free-to-be-cover-1024x606.png" alt="" width="759" height="449" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/free-to-be-cover-1024x606.png 1024w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/free-to-be-cover-300x178.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/free-to-be-cover-768x455.png 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/free-to-be-cover-400x237.png 400w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/free-to-be-cover.png 1118w" sizes="(max-width: 759px) 100vw, 759px" /><p id="caption-attachment-58301" class="wp-caption-text">Part of the cover of the book adaptation of &#8220;Free to Be&#8230; You and Me&#8221; (1974)</p></div>
<p>He writes:</p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><em>Every experimental philosopher has tons of practice doing this. It is how we answer The Question in Q&amp;As. It is how we write grant applications. It is how we pitch ourselves in cover letters for jobs: “Oh sure, if you hire me, I will be conducting empirical research, but actually this is super important to existing debates in philosophy because x, y, and z”. This is the strategy experimental philosophers advise each other to use. It is the strategy </em>I<em> advise others to use.</em></p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><em>This sort of appeasement is, by and large, what we see in the academic literature on experimental philosophy. Arguments for experimental philosophy, whether friendly or unfriendly for the armchair, have, in one way or another, positioned themselves as being in service of academic philosophy’s status quo. The stated goal is always improvement upon existing questions and not the creation of something fundamentally new.</em></p>
<p>He thinks it&#8217;s time for a change. His preferred answer to <em>The Question </em>is: &#8220;Of course we are doing philosophy. We are just not doing philosophy in their way. Not in the analytic way.&#8221;</p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><em>What if we as experimental philosophers stopped playing this game? What if we pitched experimental philosophy as something historically connected with analytic philosophy but not metaphilosophically connected to it? As its own philosophy with its own methods and its own questions? As a tradition of philosophy as legitimate of an heir to Aristotle, Leibniz, Hume, and Quine as analytic philosophy?</em></p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><em>For a lot of us, this would certainly be more truthful than how we currently pitch our work. Experimental philosophy is by no means a unified field, but a lot of its members, myself among them, just do not care about or believe in the specific problems and specific questions analytic philosophy is working on. Sure, we can pitch our research as applying to those debates. God knows I could monologue off the cuff for an hour about why my experimental research actually matters to ongoing debates in analytic philosophy. Nonetheless, if we are being honest with ourselves, those problems aren’t what motivates us. They are little more than a gateway drug introducing us to other puzzles about, e.g., knowledge, morality, or the mind. We are now interested in our own things, and they are philosophy because they are deep, deep questions about ourselves and our reality&#8230;</em></p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><em>As experimental philosophy matures and diverges from the sorts of debates it first emerged to address, we are seeing experimental philosophy becoming an independent philosophical tradition in its own right. A tradition interested in its own set of philosophical questions with its own view of how best to approach them. Consequently, experimental philosophers should celebrate and defend this against analytic philosophy’s more chauvinistic tendencies. We should be (and largely already are) proud that we are pushing the limits of what philosophy can be, shaking up stale literatures and challenging stale methodological assumptions. Why not take the extra step and openly embrace experimental philosophy [as] its own independent tradition?</em></p>
<p>You can read Landes&#8217; whole post <a href="https://ethanlandes.com/posts/2026/01/xphi-manifesto-2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.* His is one view about the reception of experimental philosophy in the profession today, and I&#8217;m wondering if it is shared, both by those who do experimental philosophy and those who do not. We can also take up the question of whether we should, as he suggests, &#8220;embrace experimental philosophy [as] its own independent tradition,&#8221; what that would mean, and how it fits with the continued broadening of what counts as &#8220;respectable&#8221; mainstream academic philosophy since the 1970s.</p>
<p>* I&#8217;m not crazy about its treatment of &#8220;analytic philosophy&#8221;, but let&#8217;s leave that aside.</p>
